From: "Jeong, Heon" <blmarket@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 21:45:00 -0700
Subject: Free the environ array setenv replaces

environ_push() points the process environ at a freshly xcalloc'd empty
array and then setenv()s each visible entry into it. libc did not
allocate that array, so the first setenv() builds an array of its own
and repoints environ at it, and the xcalloc'd one is left with nothing
pointing to it. tmux only ever calls environ_push() between fork() and
exec (job.c, spawn.c), where the loss is erased microseconds later by
the exec, which is why it never mattered - but the allocation is lost
all the same, and the transpilation's unit tests call environ_push() in
a process that lives on.

Keeping the pointer and giving the block back once environ no longer
points at it is safe on either outcome: with no visible entries environ
still points at our array and it is kept, and once any setenv() has
replaced it the comparison sees the new array and frees ours.

A harness that links the 3.7b environ.c, pushes one variable, and exits
loses the block under AddressSanitizer:

    Direct leak of 8 byte(s) in 1 object(s) allocated from:
        #1 xcalloc xmalloc.c:47
        #2 environ_push environ.c:219

and none with this applied. Not fixed upstream as of master.
---
 environ.c | 7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/environ.c b/environ.c
--- a/environ.c
+++ b/environ.c
@@ -214,15 +214,18 @@
 void
 environ_push(struct environ *env)
 {
-	struct environ_entry	*envent;
+	struct environ_entry	 *envent;
+	char			**new_environ;
 
-	environ = xcalloc(1, sizeof *environ);
+	new_environ = environ = xcalloc(1, sizeof *environ);
 	RB_FOREACH(envent, environ, env) {
 		if (envent->value != NULL &&
 		    *envent->name != '\0' &&
 		    (~envent->flags & ENVIRON_HIDDEN))
 			setenv(envent->name, envent->value, 1);
 	}
+	if (environ != new_environ)
+		free(new_environ);
 }
 
 /* Log the environment. */
